 marrow  transplant which also failed her  we had her in remission um from leukemia  within eight weeks of high dose cbd thc  in a plant-based diet she was not taking  any other treatment as the parents had  opted out  for you know anything further because  they just felt that her quality of life  was  not good with treatment she was uh  basically what we call nad no evidence  of disease for eight months  and for whatever reason i still don't  know to this day the family stopped the  oil and she did relapse  and so i have seen this before with some  other adult patients  and i usually recommend that if you get  to a point where cannabis has helped you  achieve  freedom from cancer it's probably not a  good idea to stop  another patient with stage 4  osteosarcoma similar to  the first one this is a child who came  in with a massive  pulmonary tumor metastatic tumor that  was inoperable  she had two tumors basically breaking  through in her legs just  nasty tumors you know bulging through  her legs  and we got her on high dose cannabinoids  and there was shrinkage  um immediately you know basically in the  if after six weeks  she was able she was oxygen dependent  when i met with her because of that  pulmonary tumor she was able to get off  oxygen she was taking high dose pain  meds she got off of that  she wasn't able to walk she started  walking again even  you know before kobet flew to visit a  friend in colorado  her father is an engineer  type and was really going by the cat  scans and the mris and measuring the  tumor in the lungs and we were able to  see that her overall tumor burden was  decreased by 95  after cannabis was added and  she was able to have that pulmonary  tumor it became operable because it  shrunk down to a  size that they could take out and what  they found when they  um biopsied this tumor is that  it was only five less than five percent  cancer cells but most of it was  basically dead tissue so she continues  on treatment  but uh according to her oncologist who  spoke to me  uh multiple times but basically a year  after we started her she said to me i  sent  her to you for you know hospice  palliative  uh properties and here she is a year  later with less cancer and doing great  so  there's something there we should be  free to study this  i have a two-year a two-year-old she's  not two anymore she's  taller than me now she had wilms tumor  uh she had a relapse  we had her in remission within six  months she was able to stop chemo early  and she's over  five years without  cancer and thriving and then another  young man  with stage four osteosarcoma who  cancer was progressing his oncologist  was on board to try cannabis and  we got him off opiates he started  walking again and he has con  continued on treatment with an improved  prognosis  where he overall has much less cancer in  his body than when he came to me  and happy to say his parents just  reported that he'll be starting college  uh in september of this year uh so  there's something there  and we should be studying this we should  not be prohibited this is uh  these are children who likely  would not have done well without a  cannabis in the picture  now considerations for pediatric  patients obviously benefit versus risk  always always always if i have a  patient coming in who is seizure free on  a pharmaceutical  and they just say well we just don't  like the idea of the drug and we want to  switch there are no guarantees with  cannabis  and for the most part if your child  seizure free  and you can have follow-up eegs at  some point your child may actually be  able to get off medication  um and so to me although  i think cannabis is great if you are  achieving the end goal with another  um product  or or a different medication that's  proven to work and  has those scientific studies behind it  i don't know that it's always beneficial  to switch  one of the other considerations is  certainly as you've learned all these  kids that come to me are  are um medically complex  they're usually on medication and they  can have drug drug interactions you must  be aware of that and again that's  another whole  uh talk but when we talk about products  so if you're interested to know like so  what about the products that we're using  so we always want to use concentrated  usually these are liquids like tinctures  or what we call oils  you want them to be concentrated if  there's one milligram  in a teaspoon and your child needs 200  milligrams that's not going to work  the only one that i don't like  concentrated is thc because we want to  be able to accurately  uh give low doses i want to be able to  measure out one milligram  but with cbd and the other compounds  concentrated oils allow children to take  less  volume and these products don't taste  good so it can be challenging to get  them into children  uh timing of doses is important if your  child needs every four hour dosing and  is doing great  how are we going to get your child  dosing in school right now  schools prohibit except in one  particular case in california schools  prohibit  any cannabis on campus so i have  families that have to drive in  at lunchtime take their school off  campus give them their  uh medicine and then put them back in  the classroom this disrupts the child's  day this disrupts the parents day so  this is another issue that of course we  need to resolve  we want to make sure products are  consistent batch to batch if i buy  a bottle of cbd oil that helps my child  i don't want to buy  next month's bottle and find out it's  something different  it's very important that you have that  consistency as well as a reliable source  somebody who says oh i made a batch of  oil  or gets it hey my friend gave me some  oil it's great it worked great  and now you can't get it again well  that's not great medicine right because  if you can't repeat it  that's not going to work affordability  is an issue cbd can be expensive in high  doses  and this is often why some families go  to epidiolex again because it's covered  by insurance  local access i have families that will  drive four hours in one direction to  pick up oil  a certain product for their child and  again in california now that we are  legalized  medically and for adult use one would  think that access has gotten better and  it's actually gotten worse  so that has not helped the the medical  patients  of of course it goes without saying lab  tested  and of course something that must be  understood  and accepted at the beginning of  treatment is that  uh children are growing and changing  that's what they do that's what they're  supposed to do their brain is growing  their body is growing they're changing  developing um dosing changes their  response to medications change sometimes  something works for a year and then it  doesn't work anymore this is  uh this happens with pharmaceuticals and  certainly happens with  cannabinoids and you know i joke around  that children are a moving target but  this is clearly what i've seen now often  what i get is what about the developing  brain so most studies that have looked  at  effects have looked at unregulated  chronic heavy thc  used in a non-medical fashion  that cannot be compared to a child who  may have an endo underlying  endocannabinoid system  uh dysfunction so we cannot compare its  apples and oranges  now certainly unregulated chronic heavy  thc  use in teenagers when the brain is  vulnerable  to external substances during a very  important  maturation process we do know that there  may be some risk of anxiety impulsivity  issues memory problems attention  decision making executive function  and potentially this decrease in iq and  again these studies only looking at  chronic heavy  thc use they're not looking at children  using cannabis medically  to treat a serious medical condition  in 2018 a study came out that looked at  almost 2 000 twins where their iq was  tested at age  5 12 and 18 and executive  function was tested at 18 as well and  very  interestingly they measured out who was  using cannabis and who wasn't  and with twin studies you kind of get a  baseline for that kind of genetic  and home environmental factors it kind  of is the equalizer there and what they  found and what they reported was that  lower iq and poorer executive function  was not due to cannabis use but it was  due  more likely to family background factors  which is  a very important information and again  this is in  uh people who are using cannabis  non-medically we must separate  medical from recreational use they are  not  the same in my clinical experience  children with severe  and chronic conditions taking cannabis  medicine under medical supervision  are making developmental and  neurocognitive progress  this is what i see this is what i do for  a living i see it every day  now last slide legal issues of course  always check the laws in your state or  your country  you want to make sure that if you're  treating a child you have legal approval  you want to avoid issues with child  protective services  i have a handful of patients that have  been reported  either by their physician a neurologist  or by a therapist or by  someone in the family who holds a grudge  uh and child protective services gets  involved  you want to have that legal protection  in the state of california  i am allowed to treat children with  cannabis it is state sanctions so nobody  should be getting in trouble when  they're doing it right  most states and countries if there is a  law require physician approval for  anybody under 21  and then i just wanted to list some of  the best free resources for patients so  safe access now  which is americans for safe access and  again i'm on their  policy advisory committee they're a  wonderful group that has fought for  freedom to use cannabis  medically and adult use as well  normal is the national organization for  reform of marijuana laws  excellent group who also by the way both  of these  websites have state by state information  if you're looking for  information on your particular state's  regulations and approvals for cannabis  realmscaring.org is a wonderful group  that  was involved with treating children with  epilepsy with cbd at the very beginning  from way back when and then united  patients group another  patient advocate group that can help  point you in the right direction  and then just thank you very much these  are some of the patients that i've  talked about  and so always happy to see their cute  little faces  um i appreciate your time and  hope that this lecture was able to share  with you how  i use cannabis in children with  complex medical conditions  you
 

